WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Speaker 1: A 16 year old pregnant girl in Florida&nbsp;
will not be able to get an abortion because a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.400
court has decided that she is not sufficiently&nbsp;
mature enough to decide for herself whether it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.840
the right decision to carry the fetus to term&nbsp;
or to get an abortion. This is absolute madness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.280
The girl's name is not being provided as she is&nbsp;
under 18, and they want to protect her identity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:26.400
But Florida has a new 15 week abortion ban, the&nbsp;
constitutionality of which is being questioned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:30.960
But it's staying in place for now, at least.&nbsp;
The state brought that threshold down from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:37.120
24 weeks back, a couple of months ago. And there&nbsp;
is a state law in Florida that requires minors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.880
to get parental consent for an abortion, which&nbsp;
I would argue should not be necessary, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.000
even though you're a minor, it's still your body&nbsp;
after all. It's still going to be a decision that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.120
involves you for the next 18 years plus. So it&nbsp;
seems like they only had this rule in place as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:58.880
a way to embarrass the girl who becomes pregnant,&nbsp;
to have to have her go to her parents and explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.520
the situation that she's in and try to get their&nbsp;
approval. But as it pertains to this story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:09.040
the girl in question could not get her parental&nbsp;
consent because she is parentless and she lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:13.840
with a relative and has an appointed guardian.&nbsp;
But that wasn't good enough for a circuit court&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.840
judge who denied the parental consent waiver and a&nbsp;
three judge panel upheld the decision. A statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:25.040
from the appeals court said the appellants had not&nbsp;
established by clear and convincing evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.000
she was sufficiently mature to decide whether&nbsp;
to terminate her pregnancy. So per the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.800
of Florida, the girl is not mature enough to&nbsp;
decide whether an abortion makes sense for her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:41.520
yet she is somehow mature enough to carry a baby&nbsp;
to term, give birth and raise it to adulthood.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.640
How does that make any sense at all? Something&nbsp;
tells me that being a parent requires a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.320
maturity than deciding whether a medical procedure&nbsp;
for someone in your position makes sense for you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.760
So this is just the latest example of Republican&nbsp;
barbarity on the topic, like in the lead up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.600
Roe versus Wade getting overturned when we could&nbsp;
kind of see the writing on the wall because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:06.080
Trump's Supreme Court justices. And then when&nbsp;
the draft opinion was leaked and then when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.360
was made official back in June, all along the&nbsp;
right wing assured the rest of the country that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.440
they were going to be responsible on this abortion&nbsp;
issue. If Roe got overturned, that they would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:20.000
be completely draconian in how they addressed&nbsp;
the matter. And they would say things like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.800
oh, well, we'll have exceptions for rape. Well,&nbsp;
now that's being put into question because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.680
realized how logistically challenging that would&nbsp;
be, because it could easily lead to an increase in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.480
false rape accusations. Then they'd say things&nbsp;
like, Oh, well, there'll be exceptions for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.000
life of the mother. When the life of the mother&nbsp;
is put at risk, you'll be able to get an abortion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.640
in that instance. But now they're realizing that&nbsp;
you can't always assess risk levels in this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:48.240
where it's like 100% that the mother is going&nbsp;
to survive if she has the child, or 100%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.680
chance that she's going to die if she has the&nbsp;
child, like usually it's in this gray area where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.560
you don't know with any certainty what's going&nbsp;
to happen. So who's going to decide whether or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.720
not you fit under that exception for Life of&nbsp;
the Mother? And they also used to say, Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.920
well, if Roe versus Wade gets overturned, then&nbsp;
you're going to be able to travel to a different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.320
state to get an abortion if it's illegal in your&nbsp;
state. Now we're starting to see states try to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.760
criminalize that very practice as well. So the&nbsp;
reality has been a lot more draconian than many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:20.240
of us could have even imagined. And if there's&nbsp;
any silver lining at all in the horrors that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.080
these countless women have had to deal with&nbsp;
across the country, including in this story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:28.560
it's that maybe it will incentivize&nbsp;
people to go out and vote in the fall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.560
Maybe Democrats will be able to ultimately hold on&nbsp;
to the House of Representatives. Maybe they'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.600
able to pick up a couple of seats in the Senate so&nbsp;
that we can move to eliminate the filibuster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:42.160
work to codify Roe versus Wade into law.&nbsp;
That has to be the path forward because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:48.160
we absolutely cannot trust Republicans with&nbsp;
control over this abortion issue. And sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.280
we're probably going to see plenty more cases like&nbsp;
this one out of Florida. It's absolutely sickening

